Title:
Obfuscation vs the Optimizer: An LLVM Middle-End Arms Race

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post examines how compiler middle-end optimizations (LLVM) can automatically de-obfuscate code, and why obfuscation techniques that “work” today can break with a single optimizer change tomorrow. It walks through an intentionally opaque “mystery” function and shows how LLVM passes (e.g., EarlyCSE/InstCombine) fold constant-heavy arithmetic/bitwise obfuscation down to a trivial `ret 0`. The author then strengthens the obfuscation by mixing in an unknown value (function argument), framing it as semi-linear Mixed Boolean Arithmetic (MBA) constant obfuscation, which initially resists LLVM 18. Upgrading to LLVM 19 re-breaks the obfuscation due to a specific InstCombine commit that adds De Morgan–based inversion propagation, enabling deeper simplification and cancellation (`x - x -> 0`). The post highlights the “yin-yang” relationship between obfuscation and de-obfuscation: both rely on the same algebraic reasoning and IR-level analyses. It’s useful for obfuscator authors, reverse engineers, and tool developers building deobfuscation/IR simplification pipelines.

Technical Focus:
- LLVM compilation stages (front-end/middle-end/back-end) and LLVM IR
- Optimization/de-obfuscation passes: EarlyCSE, InstCombine, Reassociate, GVN, BDCE, SimplifyCFG
- Constant folding/propagation and common subexpression elimination
- Boolean algebra canonicalization (De Morgan’s laws) enabling new simplifications
- Mixed Boolean Arithmetic (MBA) constant obfuscation and its fragility across compiler versions

Use Cases:
- Evaluating whether an IR-level obfuscation survives standard `-O2` pipelines across LLVM versions
- Building deobfuscation tooling by reusing compiler-style simplification passes/strategies
- Designing obfuscation transformations that specifically target/evade InstCombine/GVN-style reasoning
- Regression testing obfuscation against optimizer changes (commit-level “breakage” tracking)
- Training reverse engineers to recognize when “complex” expressions are optimizer-collapsible constants

Keywords:
LLVM, Clang, LLVM IR, middle-end, opt, llc, optimization passes, EarlyCSE, InstCombine, Reassociate, GVN, BDCE, constant folding, constant propagation, common subexpression elimination, De Morgan’s law, boolean algebra, Mixed Boolean Arithmetic, MBA obfuscation, deobfuscation, reverse engineering, compiler explorer, Binary Ninja